### Summary

When `LZ4BlockInputStream` is configured with `stopOnEmptyBlock = false` (the mode for reading concatenated block streams), it handles each empty block by calling `refill()` recursively. A long run of empty blocks exhausts the thread stack and throws `StackOverflowError` out of `read()` or `skip()`.

### Details

In `net.jpountz.lz4.LZ4BlockInputStream.refill()`:

```java
if (originalLen == 0 && compressedLen == 0) {
  if (check != 0) {
    throw new IOException("Stream is corrupted");
  }
  if (!stopOnEmptyBlock) {
    refill();
  } else {
    finished = true;
  }
  return;
}
```

Each well-formed empty block is 21 bytes and adds one stack frame, with no limit on nesting depth…

### Summary

When no system-installed `liblz4-java` is found, `net.jpountz.util.Native.load()` extracts the bundled native library to `java.io.tmpdir` and loads it with `System.load`. The path of the extracted library can be predicted before it is created, and the library file is opened without exclusive creation. Another local user who can write to the same temporary directory can therefore create the file first and control the code that is loaded.

